And here's the JavaScript code to create the tooltip:

```javascript
const svg = document.querySelector('svg');

svg.addEventListener('mouseover', (e) => {
  const tooltip = document.createElement('div');
  tooltip.textContent = 'This is a tooltip!';
  tooltip.style.position = 'absolute';
  tooltip.style.background = '#fff';
  tooltip.style.border = '1px solid #ddd';
  tooltip.style.padding = '5px';
  tooltip.style.borderRadius = '5px';
  tooltip.style.pointerEvents = 'none';
  tooltip.style.zIndex = '1000';

  const rect = e.target.getBoundingClientRect();
  const x = e.clientX + 10;
  const y = e.clientY + 10;

  tooltip.style.top = `${y}px`;
  tooltip.style.left = `${x}px`;

  document.body.appendChild(tooltip);

  const handleMouseLeave = () => {
    tooltip.remove();
  };

  svg.addEventListener('mouseleave', handleMouseLeave);

  const handleMouseMove = (e) => {
    const rect = e.target.getBoundingClientRect();
    const x = e.clientX + 10;
    const y = e.clientY + 10;

    tooltip.style.top = `${y}px`;
    tooltip.style.left = `${x}px`;
  };

  svg.addEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove);
});

svg.addEventListener('mouseout', () => {
  const tooltip = document.querySelector('div');
  if (tooltip) {
    tooltip.remove();
  }
});
```

This code creates a tooltip that appears when the user hovers over the SVG. The tooltip is a simple `div` element with some basic styling. When the user moves the mouse over the SVG, the tooltip follows the mouse cursor. When the user leaves the SVG, the tooltip disappears.

Note that this is a basic implementation, and you can customize the tooltip to fit your needs. You can also add more features, such as animating the tooltip or adding more content to it.

